WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:05.540
COVID-19 cases and deaths have been rising
in the past few weeks, with vaccination rates

00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:06.570
plateauing.

00:00:06.570 --> 00:00:11.490
Here in Germany, the slowing pace of inoculations
has forced authorities to find creative ways

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:13.010
to get things back on track.

00:00:13.010 --> 00:00:17.579
It’s in our hands, it depends what we do
on where this pandemic goes.

00:00:17.579 --> 00:00:20.569
It's the most urgent public health challenge
of our time.

00:00:20.569 --> 00:00:25.349
The COVID-19 pandemic has killed nearly 5
million people around the world.

00:00:25.349 --> 00:00:29.899
Vaccines can slash mortality rates - but only
when people have their jabs.

00:00:29.899 --> 00:00:31.669
And that's not always a given.

00:00:31.669 --> 00:00:34.050
Some governments don't give people a choice.

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:38.800
Others try to nudge citizens by restricting
where the unvaccinated can go.

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:41.030
Some have even offered bribes.

00:00:41.030 --> 00:00:45.020
But what if I told you that there's a country
that didn't do anything like that - and yet

00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:48.111
has one of the highest vaccination rates in
the world?

00:00:48.111 --> 00:00:51.752
When you take countries with more than 10
million people, Spain is the only big one

00:00:51.752 --> 00:00:54.950
where nearly 80 percent of people are fully
vaccinated.

00:00:54.950 --> 00:01:04.260
As of October 7, Italy only is at 69 percent,
France at 67 percent and Germany at 65 percent.

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:05.630
So how did they do it?

00:01:05.630 --> 00:01:08.670
And what can others learn from the Spanish
success story?

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:14.190
As we'll see, some reasons are specifically
Spanish, but others might work in other places.

00:01:14.190 --> 00:01:18.030
For Spain, the factors we're going to look
at created the perfect storm together.

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:20.930
I have 100%, 200% confidence in the Spanish
public health system.

00:01:20.930 --> 00:01:24.380
I think we have an incredible system, they
have treated us well.

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:29.649
That confidence - that trust - is one of the
cornerstones of Spain's successful vaccine

00:01:29.649 --> 00:01:30.649
drive.

00:01:30.649 --> 00:01:37.100
There has always been a close relationship
of the population with their family physicians,

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:39.030
with health centers.

00:01:39.030 --> 00:01:43.600
And this reflected in the dynamic of vaccination.

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:46.789
And in Spain, it's all free, and has been
for decades.

00:01:46.789 --> 00:01:50.499
Universal health care is even enshrined in
the constitution.

00:01:50.499 --> 00:01:56.289
Trust is absolutely not something that's normally
sort of delivered via text message or from

00:01:56.289 --> 00:01:58.000
a single infomercial.

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:03.310
It's built up over time from seeing that systems
are working for people, that they're generating

00:02:03.310 --> 00:02:04.439
good outcomes.

00:02:04.439 --> 00:02:08.780
This confidence in the health system translates
to trust in the vaccines it offers.

00:02:08.780 --> 00:02:14.240
A study by the Imperial College London found
that 79 percent of Spanish people trusted

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:20.300
COVID-19 vaccines, compared to 62 percent
in the US and 56 percent in France.

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:24.690
Another factor that pushed people to trust
the vaccines was the devastating first wave

00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:27.890
of the pandemic - and the way Spain got out
of it.

00:02:27.890 --> 00:02:30.290
That first wave killed nearly 50,000 people.

00:02:30.290 --> 00:02:32.750
Most of them were elderly.

00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:39.810
Almost everyone has a relative or a friend
or someone they know that experienced this

00:02:39.810 --> 00:02:44.260
kind of problem, who died in the hospital,
in the corridor of the hospital or even before

00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:45.260
going to the hospital.

00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:47.920
And this is in the mind of everyone.

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:53.480
So I believe that no one wants to have a similar
experience this year.

00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:57.010
Many of those who died had been infected by
younger relatives.

00:02:57.010 --> 00:03:01.760
Spain is a country of close family ties and
different generations often live together.

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:07.850
More than half of Spaniards in their late
20s live with their parents or grandparents.

00:03:07.850 --> 00:03:13.180
When older people got vaccinated, hospitalization
and death rates dropped dramatically.

00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:18.870
The success experienced in the nursing homes,
in the geriatric places, was incredible.

00:03:18.870 --> 00:03:19.870
Incredible!

00:03:19.870 --> 00:03:26.290
To me as an infectious disease doctor the
results were better than expected.

00:03:26.290 --> 00:03:30.280
People saw the vaccines working - and the
fact that so many had seen elderly relatives

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:33.640
die, meant they knew what was at stake.

00:03:33.640 --> 00:03:40.920
I’m getting vaccinated for my family, who
are already a bit older, they are 60 years

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:47.730
old and it's to take care of them and to be
able to have the guarantee of going home,

00:03:47.730 --> 00:03:51.180
coming back and not infecting them, or at
least that it's less strong.

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:54.360
This kind of more collective value is very
important when deciding to be vaccinated and

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:58.210
is part of the communication strategy on vaccination
in Spain.

00:03:58.210 --> 00:04:02.680
The authorities made protecting older people
part of their messaging to Spanish teenagers.

00:04:02.680 --> 00:04:05.150
Nearly 90 percent are vaccinated.

00:04:05.150 --> 00:04:08.790
For German teens, the figure is only at 35
percent.

00:04:08.790 --> 00:04:18.239
There have been very targeted campaigns to
the youngsters for promoting the vaccine:

00:04:18.239 --> 00:04:25.360
TV, sports advertisement organized by the
Ministry of Health and engaging youngsters

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:29.860
in telling all youngsters that it's important
to be vaccinated.

00:04:29.860 --> 00:04:34.230
Science suggests that when I see that most
people, particularly people who I identify

00:04:34.230 --> 00:04:39.480
with, if I see that a lot of them are doing
something, it changes attitudes because then

00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:44.200
I start to think, oh, well, maybe they know
something I missed, and I sort of don't want

00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:45.540
to be left out, either.

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:50.370
But it wasn't just the message which played
a role in getting young people jabbed.

00:04:50.370 --> 00:04:52.940
Authorities helped by making it easy to do.

00:04:52.940 --> 00:04:57.020
People are often dissuaded when they hear
about hassle factors or aren't absolutely

00:04:57.020 --> 00:04:58.500
sure this is recommended for them.

00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:03.280
And so research shows that if, for instance,
you give people an appointment and simply

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:07.341
say, you know, we've scheduled you at this
time and date in a convenient location, that

00:05:07.341 --> 00:05:09.970
can dramatically increase vaccine uptake.

00:05:09.970 --> 00:05:15.840
There were personalized phone calls to call
for the vaccination, a follow up by the family

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:18.169
physicians in the primary health care centers.

00:05:18.169 --> 00:05:25.360
There is now an active search for the rest
of the people to try to ensure that those

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:30.360
who were not able to be vaccinated because
they were traveling or they were sick or they

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:39.250
have had COVID recently are identified, are
reached by the system and we complete the

00:05:39.250 --> 00:05:40.250
vaccination.

00:05:40.250 --> 00:05:45.010
Another factor that helped Spain's jab rate
was the low level of anti-vaccine sentiment.

00:05:45.010 --> 00:05:54.090
I think all in all, there was very little
receptivity or tolerance to the discourse

00:05:54.090 --> 00:05:56.670
of conspiracy or anti-vaccine.

00:05:56.670 --> 00:06:00.330
Spain's newspapers and television stations
also played a role.

00:06:00.330 --> 00:06:04.660
Critical and science-based discussion is vital
to build trust, experts say.

00:06:04.660 --> 00:06:15.030
In all the media there was a constant scrutiny
of how well we were progressing with the vaccine,

00:06:15.030 --> 00:06:23.199
and it was not just general information, but
the granular analysis of how we were advancing

00:06:23.199 --> 00:06:29.830
with each population group in each region
and how the vaccine was being distributed

00:06:29.830 --> 00:06:32.810
and whether or not it was being applied.

00:06:32.810 --> 00:06:37.600
And that region-by-region analysis led to
a race between traditional rivals.

00:06:37.600 --> 00:06:43.130
Catalonia and Madrid, for example, they compete
and they compete not just in soccer, in football.

00:06:43.130 --> 00:06:46.110
They compete in many different things, political
issues.

00:06:46.110 --> 00:06:52.090
And once the government bought and supplied
the vaccines to every autonomous community,

00:06:52.090 --> 00:06:55.790
everyone wanted to be the first to vaccinate
more people.

00:06:55.790 --> 00:06:58.610
And that brings us to the issue of access.

00:06:58.610 --> 00:07:02.980
Spain was part of the EU common purchasing
system, so getting hold of the vaccines was

00:07:02.980 --> 00:07:04.110
never a major issue.

00:07:04.110 --> 00:07:08.020
But for other countries it was - and remains
– a huge problem.

00:07:08.020 --> 00:07:12.580
Many smaller and poorer countries were stuck
at the back of the line for vaccine contracts.

00:07:12.580 --> 00:07:16.070
Africa is the continent with the lowest rate
of vaccination.

00:07:16.070 --> 00:07:21.979
Sierra Leone, Nigeria and Cameroon have fully
vaccinated less than 1 percent of their population.

00:07:21.979 --> 00:07:25.340
The factors we've mentioned don't matter if
there's no vaccine available.

00:07:25.340 --> 00:07:30.320
But Spain was lucky - and did sensible things
in that lucky situation.

00:07:30.320 --> 00:07:39.300
So it's a happy alignment of planets or a
combination of factors: trust of the population,

00:07:39.300 --> 00:07:47.190
a reliability on the health system by the
population and on the population by the health

00:07:47.190 --> 00:07:56.460
system, good responsiveness to the calls for
vaccination and a real concerted effort of

00:07:56.460 --> 00:07:59.130
prioritizing the vaccination.

00:07:59.130 --> 00:08:04.180
Of all the factors we've considered, experts
say trust is one of the most important - and

00:08:04.180 --> 00:08:08.380
it will be trust that makes all the difference
the next time a killer virus strikes.

00:08:08.380 --> 00:08:14.419
I don't know of a quick switch we can flip
in societies where trust is lower to make

00:08:14.419 --> 00:08:19.930
it higher, but I do think this points to the
hard work we need to do in the years and decades

00:08:19.930 --> 00:08:24.290
ahead because this is not going to be our
last pandemic or our last crisis where trust

00:08:24.290 --> 00:08:26.620
in science matters.

00:08:26.620 --> 00:08:29.870
And I think we need to invest more in building
that trust and making sure it's earned.

